WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:06.329
We all know that a good story can move us,
make us feel something,

00:00:06.329 --> 00:00:09.648
teach us something,
help us be better at something.

00:00:09.648 --> 00:00:15.682
But can a story literally move us,
that is get us to move and be healthy?

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:20.682
Today, we'll learn the answer to this
question in many other best practices for

00:00:20.682 --> 00:00:22.236
what makes a good story.

00:00:22.236 --> 00:00:23.638
I'm Matt Abrahams and

00:00:23.638 --> 00:00:28.376
I teach Strategic Communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:28.376 --> 00:00:32.620
Welcome to Think Fast Talk Smart,
the podcast.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:37.783
We are so
lucky today to be joined by Paula Moya.

00:00:37.783 --> 00:00:41.048
Paula is the Dan Lee and
Laura Louise Bell,

00:00:41.048 --> 00:00:46.830
Professor of the Humanities and
a Professor of English at Stanford.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:51.470
She also is a recipient of the Dean's
Award for distinguished teaching.

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:55.178
Paula has several books on the market,
the most recent being

00:00:55.178 --> 00:01:00.970
the Social Imperative Race, Close Reading,
and Contemporary Literary Criticism.

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:05.761
For the past two years, she's been part of
a research project that investigates and

00:01:05.761 --> 00:01:10.352
validates using narrative to encourage
individuals to increase their activity

00:01:10.352 --> 00:01:12.850
levels to improve health span.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.880
Welcome, Paula, I am so
eager to work with you.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.359
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Matt, I'm so
glad to be here and

00:01:18.359 --> 00:01:21.310
to have an opportunity to chat with you.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:23.530
&gt;&gt; Great, let's get started.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:27.419
Before we get into some of the really
cool ways, you're using stories to

00:01:27.419 --> 00:01:32.480
motivate people, I'd like to start with
some general questions about stories.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:37.004
You are a narratologists,
I've never heard that term before,

00:01:37.004 --> 00:01:40.457
can you define what that means and
what do you do?

00:01:40.457 --> 00:01:45.282
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Well, narratology basically
is just a study of narratives and

00:01:45.282 --> 00:01:51.520
narrative structure, and the ways that
these things affect human perception.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:56.028
So, narratologists basically is
just someone who does the studying.

00:01:56.028 --> 00:01:59.328
I've always loved reading and
I'm captivated by stories.

00:01:59.328 --> 00:02:03.495
And so it's been my pleasure
over the last decade or so,

00:02:03.495 --> 00:02:08.474
to be able to spend time thinking
about what makes narrative work.

00:02:08.474 --> 00:02:10.697
&gt;&gt; Wow, a whole field I was unaware of.

00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:15.037
So as somebody who studied
narratives as deeply as you have,

00:02:15.037 --> 00:02:20.882
I'm curious from your perspective,
what makes a good narrative or story?

00:02:20.882 --> 00:02:26.255
&gt;&gt; I guess I'd have to say that a lot
depends on what anyone means by good,

00:02:26.255 --> 00:02:26.902
right?

00:02:26.902 --> 00:02:31.873
So [LAUGH] I love many different kinds
of stories and I know that they all draw

00:02:31.873 --> 00:02:35.731
on different kinds of elements
to give them their value.

00:02:35.731 --> 00:02:40.156
But the basic elements of a story,
we can start there, of a story or

00:02:40.156 --> 00:02:44.900
a narrative or plot, so what happens,
character's, point of view or

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.321
perspective, and
that relates to focalization.

00:02:48.321 --> 00:02:50.693
Setting, where it's located, scene,

00:02:50.693 --> 00:02:55.420
kind of basically what they're
talking about, and of course, style.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:03:00.017
So conflict is also central to any
story because there always needs to be

00:03:00.017 --> 00:03:05.010
something that kind of gets the story
going and powers the events that occur.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:09.105
So these elements will always be
present in any particular story,

00:03:09.105 --> 00:03:13.740
albeit in different combinations and
with different emphases.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:17.633
So a good story or
narrative will have all of those, but

00:03:17.633 --> 00:03:22.799
it's important to know from my
perspective that what one person defines

00:03:22.799 --> 00:03:28.063
as good may be very different from what
some other person defines as good.

00:03:28.063 --> 00:03:32.657
So what good is will be very much
influenced by what someone knows,

00:03:32.657 --> 00:03:37.181
what they value, what they love,
and also what they recognize.

00:03:37.181 --> 00:03:42.148
And this is sometimes cultural, right,
based on where someone grew up,

00:03:42.148 --> 00:03:45.928
what they are accustomed to
doing in their daily lives.

00:03:45.928 --> 00:03:50.301
And it's also sometimes learned, right,
based on what we were taught in school,

00:03:50.301 --> 00:03:52.372
right, what did our teachers tell us.

00:03:52.372 --> 00:03:56.616
Or even what someone we
admire says it's good, so

00:03:56.616 --> 00:03:59.961
maybe a preacher or a mother or an aunt.

00:03:59.961 --> 00:04:04.011
So for example,
those of us who read a lot of stories or

00:04:04.011 --> 00:04:08.961
consume a lot of narratives, and so
I count myself among those because

00:04:08.961 --> 00:04:13.472
I'm always reading or
watching some cereal or something.

00:04:13.472 --> 00:04:17.888
When we do that, we can easily become
bored by characters we proceed and

00:04:17.888 --> 00:04:21.064
stock characters for
plots that are predictable.

00:04:21.064 --> 00:04:26.760
if you read enough narratives, you start
to recognize certain particular turns.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:31.015
And we get bored by them because we've
seen them too many times before and

00:04:31.015 --> 00:04:33.198
they contain for us, no surprises.

00:04:33.198 --> 00:04:39.290
At the same time, for someone else, that
same story can be fresh and interesting.

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:44.123
So my answer kind of to like what's good,
what makes a good narrative is that

00:04:44.123 --> 00:04:48.280
a story is good if someone
somewhere values it as good.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:53.068
Now, it just might not be good for me
[LAUGH] so and I'm making a larger point

00:04:53.068 --> 00:04:57.330
about the existence of multiple
conceptions of the good.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:01.347
&gt;&gt; Certainly, I hear you talking
about several things, for example,

00:05:01.347 --> 00:05:06.055
you need to think about who your story is
for and what's going to be interesting,

00:05:06.055 --> 00:05:08.155
relevant, and exciting for them.

00:05:08.155 --> 00:05:10.698
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; And you need to be careful,

00:05:10.698 --> 00:05:15.459
I heard also of falling into
the trap of using common clichés or

00:05:15.459 --> 00:05:19.867
common ways of telling the story or
designing the story.

00:05:19.867 --> 00:05:23.296
I heard you talk about surprise,
I heard you talk about a novelty,

00:05:23.296 --> 00:05:25.929
or at least I'm applying
novelty to what you said,

00:05:25.929 --> 00:05:30.030
to really make it something that stands
out, would you agree with those?

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:31.528
&gt;&gt; I would completely agree with that.

00:05:31.528 --> 00:05:36.493
So it's kind of a mix of making
it familiar, which we like.

00:05:36.493 --> 00:05:39.263
Just as human being, psychologically,

00:05:39.263 --> 00:05:44.561
we have a kind of principle of familiarity
that drives us and also making it new,

00:05:44.561 --> 00:05:50.320
making them a little surprising, giving
us something that is not predictable.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.547
So, but this kind of goes back to
the first thing that you picked up on,

00:05:54.547 --> 00:05:58.702
which is that we always have to think
about who this narrative is for,

00:05:58.702 --> 00:06:03.379
because what's going to work is going to
be different according to who it's for.

00:06:03.379 --> 00:06:07.893
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, and that is a theme we have
heard many times on this podcast about

00:06:07.893 --> 00:06:11.375
really reflecting on our audience for
our communication.

00:06:11.375 --> 00:06:15.902
Should we approach crafting stories
meant to be read differently

00:06:15.902 --> 00:06:18.130
from those that we speak?

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:22.241
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, I really agree
with that because you know it is.

00:06:22.241 --> 00:06:27.627
If you're listening to a story, you
might need a heavier dose of repetition,

00:06:27.627 --> 00:06:31.760
to help your listener to keep
track of what is happening.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:36.250
And so you see this in sort of
storytelling cultures where there's often

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:40.884
a great deal of repetition and they
come back to certain kinds of motifs, so

00:06:40.884 --> 00:06:43.700
like a certain image or a certain color.

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.389
And if you're listening to story,
you can't go back and

00:06:46.389 --> 00:06:47.740
look something up, right?

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:48.280
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:52.996
&gt;&gt; So you need the storyteller to help
you by drawing you along with these

00:06:52.996 --> 00:06:56.168
repeated motifs, these repeated words, and

00:06:56.168 --> 00:07:01.228
also reminders of who is who and
how they are related to their characters.

00:07:01.228 --> 00:07:06.167
And I think it's probably a little
easier to have a more complex

00:07:06.167 --> 00:07:09.254
narrative in writing than listening.

00:07:09.254 --> 00:07:12.219
&gt;&gt; I really want to emphasize this point.

00:07:12.219 --> 00:07:17.300
In the work that I do, there's a really
big challenge that people have, they

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:22.472
write as if they're writing their content
to be read, and then they speak it.

00:07:22.472 --> 00:07:25.795
And it just sounds different,
it doesn't connect the same way.

00:07:25.795 --> 00:07:30.312
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; A lot of the ideas that you suggested,

00:07:30.312 --> 00:07:36.135
those are all things that can really
help a story stand out when spoken,

00:07:36.135 --> 00:07:40.406
and similarly might seem redundant or
repetitive or

00:07:40.406 --> 00:07:43.240
short sighted in a written piece.

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:46.838
So the big message is we
have to envision writing for

00:07:46.838 --> 00:07:51.359
speaking and writing for
being read is very different things and

00:07:51.359 --> 00:07:55.367
I think that point is super important
&gt;&gt; It absolutely is and

00:07:55.367 --> 00:07:59.358
I'm glad that you are articulating it so
clearly, I need to learn from you.

00:07:59.358 --> 00:08:05.023
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] No, well, I do a lot of
speaking, so I repeat myself a lot,

00:08:05.023 --> 00:08:08.049
much to my family's dismay
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] But

00:08:08.049 --> 00:08:12.666
let me just say one more thing about
that is that, always when we're

00:08:12.666 --> 00:08:17.445
thinking about communicating,
we need to think about the medium, so

00:08:17.445 --> 00:08:21.420
what is the medium through
which we are communicating?

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:26.037
So obviously, a novel is a different
medium than say a story circle where

00:08:26.037 --> 00:08:29.100
you're sitting around talking to people.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:32.984
The medium really that it makes it so
important to sort of shift how you do

00:08:32.984 --> 00:08:35.852
something if you want to communicate well
&gt;&gt; So

00:08:35.852 --> 00:08:39.218
true, I cannot tell you the number
of times I see people try to

00:08:39.218 --> 00:08:42.521
tell stories through bullet
points on PowerPoint slides.

00:08:42.521 --> 00:08:47.778
And it's the wrong medium, it's the wrong
tool to get your message across and

00:08:47.778 --> 00:08:50.100
I really appreciate that advice.

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:55.034
I'd like to turn to your research,
your research and

00:08:55.034 --> 00:08:57.432
writings focus on race and culture.

00:08:57.432 --> 00:09:01.413
Do you have any guidance on how we
can be more inclusive of diversity

00:09:01.413 --> 00:09:05.765
in the stories we choose to tell and
how we actually craft those stories?

00:09:05.765 --> 00:09:09.105
&gt;&gt; Well, I think probably the easiest way,

00:09:09.105 --> 00:09:15.127
the most simple way to be inclusive
diversity is also the most obvious way.

00:09:15.127 --> 00:09:19.624
That is to write or consume stories
about those people, places,

00:09:19.624 --> 00:09:23.548
events, and dilemmas that are for
the most part absent or

00:09:23.548 --> 00:09:27.002
underrepresented in
the mainstream of society.

00:09:27.002 --> 00:09:32.152
So they're not, for example, sufficient
representations of Latinx people in

00:09:32.152 --> 00:09:37.225
the media, television theater, the movies
and journalism, to give a true and

00:09:37.225 --> 00:09:41.642
accurate picture of the richness and
the complexity of Latino life.

00:09:41.642 --> 00:09:44.880
This is not to say that there is none.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:49.246
Just to say that there was a dearth
of such representation relative to

00:09:49.246 --> 00:09:52.570
the number of Latinx people
living in this country.

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:57.019
And unfortunately, many of the
representations of Latinx people are quite

00:09:57.019 --> 00:09:58.834
negative and stereotypical.

00:09:58.834 --> 00:10:00.645
And so not representative,

00:10:00.645 --> 00:10:06.210
the wide range of experiences of people
who are associated with that ethnicity.

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:10.331
&gt;&gt; So it certainly sounds to me like,
if you're looking to be more inclusive

00:10:10.331 --> 00:10:14.318
in the stories you tell, you need to
tell stories that include people,

00:10:14.318 --> 00:10:17.950
that by definition,
I think is what we're talking about.

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:21.204
But we also have to be sensitive
to what we represent and

00:10:21.204 --> 00:10:23.268
what we tell those stories about.

00:10:23.268 --> 00:10:27.978
So it's not just including people in
the stories, it's what those stories

00:10:27.978 --> 00:10:33.060
are about and the focus and what the
message they send beyond the story itself.

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:35.398
And that's really important for
all of us to think about.

00:10:35.398 --> 00:10:37.909
As we live in a more diverse culture,

00:10:37.909 --> 00:10:41.959
it's critical to hear the stories and
voices of every one.

00:10:41.959 --> 00:10:46.690
You were part of a very cool
interdisciplinary team looking at the role

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:50.459
of narrative in motivating
positive health behavior,

00:10:50.459 --> 00:10:54.650
can you tell us briefly
about the Perfecto Project?

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:59.182
&gt;&gt; So the Perfecto Project is actually
an extension of a larger project

00:10:59.182 --> 00:11:04.560
involving a very large interdisciplinary
team at Stanford University.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:09.287
There has been researching the
effectiveness in motivating mobility of

00:11:09.287 --> 00:11:13.705
narrative based feedback that is
delivered on the ambient screen of

00:11:13.705 --> 00:11:15.344
an Android smartphone.

00:11:15.344 --> 00:11:20.060
So basically, that just means it's
a mobile mobility app that helps

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:22.592
takes the user through a narrative.

00:11:22.592 --> 00:11:28.606
And as the user exercises or walks or
does some kind of physical activity,

00:11:28.606 --> 00:11:34.044
the story moves along, so
they help power the story in that sense.

00:11:34.044 --> 00:11:36.730
&gt;&gt; It sounds to me like
it's a fitness tracker,

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:40.266
the data from which actually
moves you through a story, and

00:11:40.266 --> 00:11:44.529
that story serves to help motivate
even more movement, is that right?

00:11:44.529 --> 00:11:45.699
&gt;&gt; That's exactly right.

00:11:45.699 --> 00:11:49.959
And so what we're trying to do is create
a narrative that's interesting enough that

00:11:49.959 --> 00:11:53.509
the person wants to do their exercise so
they can get the next chapter.

00:11:53.509 --> 00:11:56.169
&gt;&gt; Wow, I think for the listeners,

00:11:56.169 --> 00:12:02.154
this notion of thinking about creating
stories that you share as a leader,

00:12:02.154 --> 00:12:06.718
as a participant of teams
that are culturally relevant.

00:12:06.718 --> 00:12:11.698
And the fact that you are finding that
the notion that a story can serve as

00:12:11.698 --> 00:12:17.320
motivation for any behavior, potentially,
is really a fascinating one.

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:22.313
As part of the work you just described,
I read that there seems to be

00:12:22.313 --> 00:12:27.790
a link between motivation and
empathy for characters in stories.

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:32.014
Can you describe this association and
any guidance that it might suggest for

00:12:32.014 --> 00:12:36.530
those of us trying to motivate
others through the stories we tell?

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:39.982
&gt;&gt; Well, I do think empathy,
where we feel for others,

00:12:39.982 --> 00:12:45.354
as well as things like recognition, where
we maybe see characters that remind us of

00:12:45.354 --> 00:12:50.830
ourselves are very important for
how stories affect listeners and readers.

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:55.321
Because one very basic psychological
phenomenon is familiarity,

00:12:55.321 --> 00:13:00.045
which is simply a form of remembering and
with the situation and event,

00:13:00.045 --> 00:13:04.910
a person, a place to provokes
a subjective feeling of recognition.

00:13:04.910 --> 00:13:09.144
So if I guess,
I've been at that tamale making party,

00:13:09.144 --> 00:13:14.865
I've been at that birthday party where
there's where we break a piñata.

00:13:14.865 --> 00:13:20.670
So it doesn't have to be perfect, but it
brings something out of ourselves to us.

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:24.726
And I think being recognized makes
us feel good, feel valued and

00:13:24.726 --> 00:13:27.590
makes us feel a sense of belonging.

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:32.338
&gt;&gt; This notion of helping people
feel what what you're talking

00:13:32.338 --> 00:13:36.260
about is familiar to them
is really interesting.

00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:41.628
It really speaks to the need to
demonstrate that you understand

00:13:41.628 --> 00:13:46.592
people's experience and
help them see how they can change

00:13:46.592 --> 00:13:51.062
that experience,
enhance that experience, etc.

00:13:51.062 --> 00:13:55.805
So this notion of using empathy
to demonstrate familiarity,

00:13:55.805 --> 00:13:58.814
I think is a really interesting tool for

00:13:58.814 --> 00:14:03.479
those of us who craft communications
to have in our toolkit.

00:14:03.479 --> 00:14:07.214
Do you have a favorite tool
that you listen for or look for

00:14:07.214 --> 00:14:09.214
in what you write or you read?

00:14:09.214 --> 00:14:11.246
For example, I do, I love alliteration.

00:14:11.246 --> 00:14:16.023
I'm curious, do you have any tools
you find that you gravitate towards

00:14:16.023 --> 00:14:18.901
in narratives that you write or listen to?

00:14:18.901 --> 00:14:21.424
&gt;&gt; That's a very interesting question.

00:14:21.424 --> 00:14:26.153
I'm not sure that I think of it as
programmatically as that, like,

00:14:26.153 --> 00:14:31.598
these are my tools, but I definitely use
alliteration, I find it very effective.

00:14:31.598 --> 00:14:36.182
So a modicum of repetition, alliteration,

00:14:36.182 --> 00:14:44.150
I will often speak my written sentences
aloud to see how they sound to me.

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:47.237
And then of course thinking about images,

00:14:47.237 --> 00:14:52.983
I'm very attentive to the use of certain
words and also not reinforcing certain

00:14:52.983 --> 00:14:58.489
associations that has been negative,
for instance, for people of color.

00:14:58.489 --> 00:15:03.630
&gt;&gt; The tools you shared are really
helpful for all of us to think about.

00:15:03.630 --> 00:15:08.109
Before we get to our closing,
I am curious if you have any last best

00:15:08.109 --> 00:15:12.840
practices on how to craft better
stories to motivate our audiences?

00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:18.581
&gt;&gt; Clearly, an author or storyteller
needs to create compelling situations and

00:15:18.581 --> 00:15:23.594
dilemmas full of suspense and surprise,
because those are things that give us joy.

00:15:23.594 --> 00:15:27.978
They also need to create characters
in whom we are interested in,

00:15:27.978 --> 00:15:32.601
whether that'd be because the characters
we see ourselves in them or

00:15:32.601 --> 00:15:36.124
because we care about them or
feel empathy for them.

00:15:36.124 --> 00:15:40.912
Or because they operate in a field of
action that in some way intrigues us,

00:15:40.912 --> 00:15:42.390
maybe even scares us.

00:15:43.390 --> 00:15:44.100
&gt;&gt; Before we end,

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:47.660
I'd like to ask you the same three
questions I asked everybody, sound okay?

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:49.944
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sure.

00:15:49.944 --> 00:15:54.818
&gt;&gt; All right, if you were to capture
the best communication advice

00:15:54.818 --> 00:15:59.962
you ever received as a five to seven
word presentation slide title,

00:15:59.962 --> 00:16:01.332
what would it be?

00:16:01.332 --> 00:16:04.259
&gt;&gt; Make them want to turn the page.

00:16:04.259 --> 00:16:05.385
&gt;&gt; I really liked that.

00:16:05.385 --> 00:16:07.536
&gt;&gt; It is an eight word sentence, I think.

00:16:07.536 --> 00:16:11.217
But if you're reading a novel, you want to
know either because the character or

00:16:11.217 --> 00:16:14.690
the situation, whatever you want to know,
what's going to happen.

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:17.683
&gt;&gt; Absolutely,
it's building suspense and intrigue, and

00:16:17.683 --> 00:16:20.310
that's important in all communication.

00:16:20.310 --> 00:16:22.587
Let me ask you question number two.

00:16:22.587 --> 00:16:26.230
Who is a communicator that you admire and
why?

00:16:26.230 --> 00:16:30.478
&gt;&gt; I would say that Margaret Atwood
is an amazing communicator,

00:16:30.478 --> 00:16:35.390
she is an amazing novelist,
I eagerly seek out anything she writes.

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:38.697
She is a beautiful user of language,

00:16:38.697 --> 00:16:45.230
she could make me envision the world
that she was putting together.

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:50.271
And she also was not afraid to make
difficult characters who are real

00:16:50.271 --> 00:16:55.660
in the sense that people like them
exist out there in the world.

00:16:55.660 --> 00:16:56.812
&gt;&gt; Final question,

00:16:56.812 --> 00:17:03.160
what are the first three ingredients that
go into a successful communication recipe?

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:08.545
&gt;&gt; I'd say sense, the story has to
make sense, humor, and then finally,

00:17:08.545 --> 00:17:13.329
suspense, and that comes from
making them want to turn the page.

00:17:13.329 --> 00:17:16.222
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I like this notion of it, it just has

00:17:16.222 --> 00:17:21.070
to make sense, right, a lot of us don't
take a simple criterion like that.

00:17:21.070 --> 00:17:24.403
We've definitely on this podcast talked
about humor and the value of humor.

00:17:24.403 --> 00:17:27.873
And you've illuminated today,
how suspense and

00:17:27.873 --> 00:17:31.100
familiarity can be really, really helpful.

00:17:32.170 --> 00:17:35.188
Paula, thank you so much for
joining us today and

00:17:35.188 --> 00:17:40.250
sharing the story of your work, and
how to make our stories work better.

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:44.312
We're taking away so many important
insights that can really strengthen our

00:17:44.312 --> 00:17:48.136
narratives and help us to motivate
people in the direction that we want.

00:17:48.136 --> 00:17:51.319
I appreciate your time and
your insights, thank you.

00:17:51.319 --> 00:17:58.163
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you, Matt, I'm very happy
to have had the chance to talk with you.

